course: everyday
lesson: 327
title: Toy age labels, cords, pretend play, card games, motion games, hammers and paintbrushes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A toy marked «for ages 3 and up» (3+) is not safe for a toddler: the age label warns that small parts or other dangers can hurt young children.
explain: Strings and cords, like a yo-yo string or a pull-toy cord, can strangle a baby, so keep them away from babies and never put a cord around anyone's neck.
explain: Games are fun when they are fair: in a card game like Uno or Snap you shuffle well, deal fairly and do not look at others' cards; in a strategy game you think, but do not keep everyone waiting; in a game of chance luck decides, so nobody is to blame.
explain: Example: a girl in Toronto playing a motion dancing game on a console clears the space around her and puts on the wrist strap, so she does not hit the lamp or send the controller flying.
explain: Real tools need care: when hammering a nail with a parent, hold the nail low and start with light taps; when cleaning paintbrushes, rinse watercolour and acrylic in water, but oil paint needs an adult and a special cleaner.
fact: Read a toy's age label: a toy marked «3 and up» should not be given to a toddler, because it may have small parts that choke.
fact: Keep yo-yos, strings and cords away from babies and never put a cord around anyone's neck, because cords can strangle.
fact: Keep ride-on toys and scooters away from pools and stairs, because a little child can roll into the water or fall down the steps.
fact: When you play pretend, like a toy kitchen or a doctor's kit, with a younger child, let them lead and join in their story; pretend play helps them learn about the world.
fact: In a card game like Uno or Snap, shuffle well, deal fairly and do not look at the other players' cards.
fact: In a strategy game, take time to think but do not keep everyone waiting too long; some games use a timer.
fact: In a game of chance, like a dice game, luck decides, not cleverness, so do not blame yourself or others for losing.
fact: Before a motion game like dancing or sports on a console, clear the space around you and wear the wrist strap.
fact: When hammering a nail with a parent, hold the nail low, start with light taps and keep your fingers away.
fact: Rinse watercolour and acrylic brushes in water; cleaning oil paint brushes needs an adult and a special cleaner, as some cleaners are poisonous.
exercise: Your aunt wants to give your 18-month-old brother a toy marked «ages 3 and up». What do you say? | It is not for toddlers, the label warns about dangers | It is fine, labels are only about how hard the toy is to understand | It is fine if someone watches for a moment | It is fine if the box is bright and colourful
exercise: What does an age label like «3+» on a toy warn about? | Choking and other dangers for young children | How clever and grown-up a child must be to enjoy it | The price of the toy in different shops | How many children can play with it at once
exercise: A yo-yo with a long string is lying near a baby. What should you do? | Move it out of the baby's reach | Let the baby hold the string to play | Tie the string around the baby's wrist | Wrap the string around the baby's neck loosely
exercise: Why must cords and strings be kept away from babies? | Cords can strangle | Cords make babies sneeze | Babies cannot see thin strings | Strings carry more germs than toys
exercise: Your little cousin is riding a ride-on toy at the pool party. What is the safe rule? | Keep the ride-on toy away from the pool and stairs | Let her ride along the pool edge to cool down | Push her fast so she stays balanced | Let her ride only on the wet tiles
exercise: Your little sister wants to play with her toy kitchen and asks you to join. What is best? | Let her lead and join in her story | Take over and show her the right way | Tell her pretend play is for babies | Cook for her while she watches
exercise: Why is pretend play with a toy kitchen or doctor's kit good for little children? | It helps them learn about the world | It stops them from ever getting ill | It teaches them to read quickly | It keeps them from making friends
exercise: You are dealing cards for a game of Uno with friends. What is fair? | Shuffle well, deal fairly and do not look at others' cards | Deal yourself the best cards first, since you're the one doing the work | Peek at one card each to be friendly | Deal quickly without shuffling at all
exercise: In a long strategy game, a boy takes ten minutes for every move. What should he do? | Think, but not keep everyone waiting too long | Take as long as he likes, it is his turn | Make random moves to go fast | Ask someone else to play for him
exercise: In a dice game, you lose three times in a row. What is the sensible thought? | Luck decides in this game, nobody is to blame | I must be bad at thinking and should practise harder | My friend must be cheating somehow | The dice do not like me today
exercise: Before playing a motion dancing game on a console, what should you do? | Clear the space and wear the wrist strap | Stand close to the television to see better | Turn off the lights to feel the music | Hold the controller loosely so you can wave freely
exercise: Why wear the wrist strap in a motion sports game? | So the controller cannot fly out of your hand | So the game can count your steps | So your hand does not get cold | So the console knows your name
exercise: You are hammering a nail into wood with your dad. How do you hold the nail? | Low, starting with light taps and fingers away | High up at the top of the nail, with big hard hits to drive it in fast | By the point, with your hand on the wood | Between your lips while you aim
exercise: Why start with light taps when using a hammer and nails? | Missing the nail hurts your fingers | Hard hits make the wood too soft | Light taps make the nail grow longer | Hammers break if used hard at first
exercise: How do you clean a brush after using watercolour or acrylic paint? | Rinse it in water | Leave it to dry with the paint on | Wipe it on your clothes | Soak it in a cleaner from the garage on your own
exercise: Why does cleaning oil paint brushes need an adult? | It needs a special cleaner, and some are poisonous | Oil paint brushes are too heavy and sticky for children to hold | Oil paint brushes cost more than other brushes | Oil paint dissolves in plain cold water
test: Which toy is the best present for a one-year-old? | A big soft toy with sewn-on eyes | A box of marbles marked «3+» | A small car with removable wheels | A set of tiny building bricks
test: A babysitter hangs a toy on a long ribbon around a baby's neck. What is true? | It is dangerous, cords around the neck can strangle | It is fine, because ribbons are much softer than cords or strings | It is fine if the toy is light | It is safe while the baby is awake
test: Which is true about a scooter near a swimming pool? | A little child can roll into the water | Scooters roll more slowly on wet tiles | Pools are the best place to learn | Water stops wheels from sliding
test: You and your sister play snap. You see her cards in the mirror behind her. What is honest? | Look away and play as normal | Use it only if you are losing | Tell her the cards you saw later | Peek once, that is not really cheating
test: Which game is decided mostly by luck? | Snakes and ladders, played with a die | Chess | A crossword puzzle | A spelling quiz
test: A boy swings his arm in a tennis game on the console and the controller flies into the TV. What would have prevented it? | Wearing the wrist strap and clearing space | Playing with the sound turned right up so you can hear the ball | Standing nearer to the screen | Using only one finger to hold it
test: Your four-year-old cousin is playing doctor and tells you that you are the patient. What is best? | Be the patient and follow her story | Explain that real doctors do it differently | Take the kit and be the doctor yourself | Say the game is silly and go away
test: Which is true about cleaning paintbrushes? | Watercolour rinses out in water, oil paint needs special cleaner | All paint washes out in plain water if you rinse it long enough | All brushes need the strong garage cleaner | Paint does not harm brushes left to dry
explain: A toddler in Ohio wrapped a yo-yo string around his neck while playing; his sister saw it and pulled it off, and their mum moved all strings out of reach.
explain: A boy in England tried to use a hammer without an adult; he missed and hit his thumb so hard it swelled and the nail went black.
fact: Cords and strings around the neck can strangle a small child in seconds.
fact: Hammers and tools cause many hand injuries at home.
test: baby wants my yo-yo | Keep it away; the string can strangle | Let him have it | Let him if you watch | Cut the string shorter
test: little bro's playing on his trike by the pool | Move him away from the pool | Let him, he's careful | Watch from inside | Tie the trike up
test: losing at dice, it's not fair im smarter | Dice is luck; don't blame others | Cheat to win | Quit and sulk | Take the dice home
test: did u hear a baby got a yo-yo string round her neck in the cot and "turned blue before her mum got there". lesson? | Keep cords and strings away from babies | Strings are fine if they're short and the baby's asleep | Only thick ropes are dangerous | Babies can untangle themselves
test: my cousin says a 2-year-old got a 3+ toy for Christmas and "swallowed 6 bits". what's the true bit | Toys marked 3+ may have small parts that choke toddlers | Age labels are just there to tell the shop what price to charge | Small parts are fine for 2-year-olds | Toddlers never swallow toys
test: kids say the boy who took ages to move at chess is "slow and dumb". pass it on? | No, that's unkind; just use a timer if needed | Yes, he's slow | Yes, and refuse to play him | Yes, tell everyone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a toy marked «3 and up»? | It should not be given to a toddler, as it may have small parts | It is designed for three players or more, and cannot be played alone | It costs three pounds or more | It requires three batteries
exam: Identify the principal reason for which oil paint brushes must be cleaned by an adult. | Some special cleaners are poisonous | Oil paint cannot be washed off at all | Children's hands stain permanently | Water damages oil paint brushes
exam: It is given that a motion game is to be played upon a console. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | Clearing the space around and wearing the wrist strap | Playing close to a glass cabinet, so that the screen can be seen clearly | Holding the controller loosely | Standing on a chair for a better view
exam: State what decides the winner in a game of chance. | Luck, not cleverness | The age of the player | The cleverest strategy | The number of players
